        "intro": "An **identity** is true wherever both sides are defined. Identities let you simplify a trigonometric expression to a shorter form. The usual plan is to write everything in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, then tidy up.",
        "facts": [
"**Reciprocal identities**: $\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}$ when $\\sin\\theta \\neq 0$, and $\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta}$ when $\\cos\\theta \\neq 0$. Also, $\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta}$ when tangent is defined and non-zero.",
"**Quotient identities**: $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}$ when $\\cos\\theta \\neq 0$, and $\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}$ when $\\sin\\theta \\neq 0$. At $\\theta = 90^\\circ$, cotangent is zero while tangent is undefined, so use the quotient definition of cotangent.",
"**Pythagorean identity**: $\\sin^{2}\\theta + \\cos^{2}\\theta = 1$, which rearranges to $1 - \\sin^{2}\\theta = \\cos^{2}\\theta$ and $1 - \\cos^{2}\\theta = \\sin^{2}\\theta$.",
"**Secondary Pythagorean identities**: $1 + \\tan^{2}\\theta = \\sec^{2}\\theta$ and $1 + \\cot^{2}\\theta = \\operatorname{cosec}^{2}\\theta$, so $\\sec^{2}\\theta - \\tan^{2}\\theta = 1$ and $\\operatorname{cosec}^{2}\\theta - \\cot^{2}\\theta = 1$.",
"The algebra is ordinary: factorise common terms, expand brackets, use the difference of two squares, and put fractions over a common denominator."
],
